# HTML script closing tag in template literals

When building HTML strings in Node (e.g. for `res.send()` or injected into a page) that include `<script>...</script>` blocks inside a **JavaScript template literal**, the closing tag must not be written literally as `</script>` inside the same template. Doing so ends the *surrounding* script block in the source file. Escaping like `<\\/script>` or `<\/script>` produces a **literal backslash** in the output, so the browser never sees the sequence `</script>` and does not close the script — the rest of the page is parsed as script content, breaking layout and preventing later scripts from running.

**Fix:** close script tags via a variable (or concatenation) so the string sent to the client contains the exact characters `</script>`:

```javascript
const S = '</script>';
// In template:
`<script>...</script>${S}
...
<script>...</script>${S}`
```

Or: `` `...` + '</script>' + `...` ``. See `src/admin/agent/agentPageContent.js` (e.g. `PRISM_TAIL`, `S`) for the pattern used in this repo.
